window.SAT_DATA = window.SAT_DATA || {};
(window.SAT_DATA.rwBank = window.SAT_DATA.rwBank || []).push(
{"id": "rw-tra-a1", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "For decades, scholars assumed that the Inka khipu—assemblages of knotted cords used across the Andes—served purely as accounting instruments, tallying census counts and tribute obligations in a base-ten positional code. ______ a subset of surviving khipus exhibits knot patterns far too irregular to be arithmetic, leading some researchers to argue that these devices also encoded narrative or genealogical records."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "For example,", "why": "Frames the second sentence as an instance of accounting use, but the irregular, non-arithmetic khipus are a counterexample to that use, not an illustration of it.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "However,", "why": "The first sentence reports a long-held assumption that khipu were 'purely' accounting tools; the second presents evidence (patterns too irregular to be arithmetic) that undercuts it, so a contrastive marker is required."}, {"text": "As a result,", "why": "Implies the irregular knot patterns are caused by scholars' accounting assumption, a causal link the text never establishes.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Likewise,", "why": "Signals that the second sentence continues in the same direction as the first, but it reverses the first sentence's claim rather than paralleling it.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-tra-a2", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "During slow-wave sleep, the hippocampus replays sequences of neural firing first recorded during waking experience, a process believed to transfer fragile new memories into durable neocortical storage. These replayed sequences are frequently compressed in time, unspooling far faster than the events that generated them. ______ a maze run that took a rat several seconds to complete may be reactivated in a small fraction of that time."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "Nevertheless,", "why": "Signals a concession or reversal, but the maze sentence agrees with and illustrates the preceding claim rather than qualifying it.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Consequently,", "why": "Treats the rat-maze reactivation as a downstream effect of temporal compression, but it is a concrete instance of that compression, not a separate result.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "In contrast,", "why": "Signals opposition, yet the maze example points in the same direction as the general claim it follows.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "For example,", "why": "The prior sentence makes a general claim—replayed sequences are time-compressed, faster than the original events—and the rat-maze sentence supplies a concrete instance of exactly that, so exemplification fits."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-tra-a3", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Practitioners of kintsugi mend broken ceramics by rejoining the shards with lacquer that has been dusted or mixed with powdered gold, so that the healed seams gleam rather than vanish. The technique pointedly refuses to conceal a vessel's history of breakage. ______ it makes the fracture the most conspicuous feature of the restored object, treating the flaw as ornament rather than defect."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "Indeed,", "why": "The second sentence says the technique refuses to hide breakage; the third goes further—making the fracture the object's most conspicuous feature—so an intensifying marker that strengthens the same point is required."}, {"text": "Nevertheless,", "why": "Signals a reversal, but the third sentence escalates the second's point rather than contradicting it.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "By contrast,", "why": "Signals opposition between the sentences, yet both describe the same refusal to disguise damage, only more emphatically.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "In the meantime,", "why": "Marks a temporal relationship, but the sentences describe no sequence of events in time.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-tra-a4", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "When a person loses a hand, the strip of somatosensory cortex once devoted to processing signals from that hand does not simply fall silent. Adjacent territories—those that map the face and the upper arm—gradually encroach on the vacated region. ______ lightly touching an amputee's cheek can evoke the vivid, precisely located sensation of a missing finger being stroked."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "For instance,", "why": "Casts the cheek phenomenon as an example of the cortical encroachment, but touching the cheek is a perceptual effect that follows from the rewiring, not an instance of the rewiring itself.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "However,", "why": "Signals contrast, but the third sentence follows from the second rather than opposing it.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "As a result,", "why": "Because the face map has encroached on the hand's former cortical territory, stimulating the cheek now activates that region and produces a phantom finger sensation—a direct causal consequence."}, {"text": "Likewise,", "why": "Signals a parallel between two comparable situations, but the third sentence reports an outcome of the second, not a similar case.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-tra-a5", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The body of geographic knowledge encoded in Aboriginal Australian songlines—the locations of water sources, seasonal foods, and safe passages across hundreds of miles of terrain—is so vast that no conventional atlas could easily contain it. A single trained custodian, ______, can retrieve any portion of it by mentally singing the verses in sequence, each one anchored to a landmark passed along the route."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "therefore,", "why": "Implies the knowledge's vastness causes one person's mastery of it, but the text presents no such causal link—if anything, vastness would make mastery harder.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "nevertheless,", "why": "The first sentence stresses that the knowledge is too vast for an atlas, setting up an expectation that a single person could not hold it; the second overturns that expectation, so a concessive marker is required."}, {"text": "moreover,", "why": "Signals a mere addition of another point in the same direction, but the relationship is concessive—the second sentence defies the expectation the first raises rather than reinforcing it.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "for instance,", "why": "Presents the custodian as an example of the knowledge's vastness, but a person who holds the knowledge is not an instance of the knowledge itself.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-tra-c1", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Nitinol, an alloy of nickel and titanium, can \"remember\" a preset shape: bend it at room temperature and it snaps back the instant it is heated past a transition point. Because ordinary metals lose strength as repeated loading accumulates microscopic damage, engineers long assumed that a nitinol component's recovery force would degrade steadily over its service life. ______ specimens cycled through tens of thousands of deformations showed almost no decline in that force."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "However,", "why": "The finding (almost no decline) directly contradicts the engineers' assumption that the force would degrade steadily, so the sentence marks an opposition."}, {"text": "Consequently,", "why": "Asserts that the finding follows from the assumption as a cause-effect result, but the finding opposes the assumption rather than flowing from it.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Indeed,", "why": "Signals that the finding confirms or intensifies the prior claim; a hasty reader expects confirmation, but the data refute the assumption instead.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Likewise,", "why": "Signals a parallel second case sharing the same pattern, yet no comparable case is offered and the relation is contradiction, not similarity.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-tra-c2", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Scholars of Gerard Manley Hopkins emphasize that his \"sprung rhythm\" counts stressed syllables rather than total syllables, so lines of very different length can register as metrically equivalent. The system also lets Hopkins string together consecutive stressed monosyllables for a hammering, percussive effect. ______ the opening of \"The Windhover\" drives five heavy beats through its first handful of words, a density all but impossible in orthodox iambic verse."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "Moreover,", "why": "Signals a further, separate point, but the sentence supplies a concrete illustration of the immediately preceding claim rather than adding a new one.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Consequently,", "why": "Marks an effect, yet the Windhover line is not caused by the description of the technique; it demonstrates it.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "For example,", "why": "The Windhover opening is a specific instance of the general claim that Hopkins packs consecutive stressed monosyllables for percussive effect."}, {"text": "Nevertheless,", "why": "Signals contrast, but the line supports the prior statement rather than qualifying or opposing it.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-tra-c3", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Silica aerogel is roughly 95 percent air by volume; the sparse solid silica that remains forms a tortuous, branching lattice that severely impedes the flow of heat. ______ engineers at NASA have packed the material around the electronics of Mars rovers to keep the components from freezing during the planet's brutally cold nights."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "Nonetheless,", "why": "Signals contrast, but using a heat-blocking material as insulation is fully consistent with the property just described, not opposed to it.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "As a result,", "why": "The lattice's impeding of heat flow is the reason engineers use aerogel to insulate the rovers; the application is a consequence of the property."}, {"text": "For instance,", "why": "Frames the rover use as one illustration of impeding heat flow, but it is a downstream application motivated by that property, not an instance of the property itself.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Likewise,", "why": "Signals a parallel similar case, yet nothing comparable is set alongside the aerogel to establish similarity.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-tra-c4", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The Old English epic Beowulf binds each line together not through end rhyme but through alliteration: the stressed syllables of the first half-line share an initial sound with the first stress of the second half-line. A translator who wants to preserve this music must therefore choose words to satisfy a constraint of sound before one of sense. ______ the translator's first question about a given word is often not what it means but how it begins."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "However,", "why": "Signals contrast, but the final sentence agrees with and clarifies the prior claim rather than opposing it.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "For example,", "why": "Promises a concrete instance, yet the sentence offers no specific word or line, only a general rephrasing of the constraint.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By comparison,", "why": "Signals that two things are being weighed against each other, but nothing is being compared here.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "In other words,", "why": "The closing sentence restates the preceding point—sound before sense—in plainer terms, the hallmark of a clarifying restatement."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-tra-c5", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Carbon-fiber-reinforced polymer has become a staple of aerospace design because it is far lighter than aluminum yet nearly as stiff. ______ it does not corrode as metal airframes do, sparing operators the costly inspections and treatments that rust and fatigue cracking demand. Together, these properties account for the material's rapid spread through modern airliner fleets."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "For example,", "why": "Corrosion resistance is not an instance of being lighter and stiffer; it is a distinct property, so it cannot illustrate the prior claim.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Moreover,", "why": "The sentence adds a second, independent advantage (corrosion resistance) to the first (light yet stiff), which \"Together, these properties\" then sums up."}, {"text": "However,", "why": "Signals contrast, but both sentences describe advantages of the material, so there is no opposition.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "As a result,", "why": "Marks an effect, yet resistance to corrosion is not a consequence of the material's being light and stiff.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-tra-b1", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The inscriptions of the Indus Valley civilization are frustratingly brief, averaging just five signs apiece, and no bilingual text like the Rosetta Stone has surfaced to anchor a translation. Some researchers take this scarcity of evidence to mean that the symbols never encoded language at all, serving instead as heraldic or economic marks. ______ the computational linguist Rajesh Rao has shown that the signs' statistical patterning—their conditional entropy—closely resembles that of known scripts rather than that of nonlinguistic sign systems."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "Therefore,", "why": "Sentence 3 is not a consequence of the skeptics' claim in sentence 2; Rao's finding contradicts that claim, so no cause-and-effect link exists.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By contrast,", "why": "Sentence 2 reports skeptics who conclude the symbols 'never encoded language'; sentence 3 presents Rao's finding that the patterning 'resembles that of known scripts,' which opposes that conclusion, so the transition must mark opposition."}, {"text": "Likewise,", "why": "This treats Rao as adding to the skeptics' view, but his entropy result argues the opposite—that the signs behave like language.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "For example,", "why": "Rao's finding is not an instance of the skeptics' claim; it undercuts it, so an exemplification transition is unsupported.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-rhs-a1", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["Coral reefs worldwide have declined sharply due to warming-induced bleaching.", "In a 2021 study, marine biologist Lena Okafor tested \"larval seeding\"—releasing coral larvae onto degraded reef patches—off the coast of the Philippines.", "On seeded reef patches, coral cover increased by 34% over two years.", "On unseeded control patches, coral cover increased by only 5%.", "Larval seeding is labor-intensive and is currently feasible only for small reef areas.", "Corals established through seeding were more genetically diverse than corals from natural recovery."]}, "stem": "The student wants to emphasize a limitation of larval seeding as a restoration method. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "Coral reefs around the world have declined sharply because of warming-induced bleaching.", "why": "Restates the background problem the method addresses; it is general context, not a limitation of larval seeding itself.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "Although larval seeding raised coral cover far more than natural recovery did, it is labor-intensive and currently feasible only for small reef areas.", "why": "Places the constraint from the notes—\"labor-intensive\" and \"feasible only for small reef areas\"—in the main clause, foregrounding the limitation while conceding the benefit, exactly as the goal requires."}, {"text": "Corals established through larval seeding were more genetically diverse than corals produced by natural recovery.", "why": "This states an advantage of seeding, the opposite of a limitation, so it works against the stated goal.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "On patches seeded with coral larvae, coral cover rose 34% over two years, compared with just 5% on unseeded patches.", "why": "Accurately reports the study's headline result showing the method's effectiveness, but effectiveness is not a limitation.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-rhs-a2", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["Congestion pricing charges drivers a fee to enter a city's central district during peak hours.", "In 2019, urban economist Priya Nair studied a mid-sized European city that had introduced a peak-hour charge.", "Traffic entering the central district fell by 22% within the first year.", "Average bus travel times in the district improved by 11%.", "Before the charge, district retailers feared it would reduce foot traffic and cut their sales.", "Survey data showed that district retail sales were unchanged one year after the charge took effect."]}, "stem": "The student wants to reassure an audience concerned about the charge's effect on local businesses. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "Within the first year of the peak-hour charge, traffic entering the central district fell by 22%.", "why": "Reports a traffic outcome, which does not address the audience's specific worry about local businesses.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Congestion pricing charges drivers a fee to enter a city's central district during peak hours.", "why": "Defines the policy in general terms and says nothing about its effect on businesses, so it cannot reassure the audience.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "Before the charge took effect, district retailers feared that it would reduce foot traffic and cut into their sales.", "why": "States the retailers' fear without resolving it, which would heighten rather than ease the audience's concern.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Although district retailers had feared the charge would hurt their sales, survey data showed that retail sales were unchanged a year after it took effect.", "why": "Pairs the retailers' fear (bullet 5) with the finding that sales were \"unchanged\" (bullet 6), directly answering the business concern with reassuring evidence."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-rhs-a3", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["Antarctic krill are small crustaceans that feed on phytoplankton near the ocean surface.", "After feeding, krill descend to deeper water and excrete carbon-rich fecal pellets.", "These sinking pellets carry carbon from the surface into the deep ocean, where it can be stored for centuries.", "In a 2022 study, oceanographer Marcus Feld estimated that Southern Ocean krill sequester about 23 million tons of carbon per year.", "Krill populations have declined in parts of the Southern Ocean as sea ice has retreated.", "Feld cautioned that his estimate did not account for regional variation in krill density."]}, "stem": "The student wants to explain how krill contribute to carbon storage in the deep ocean. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "After feeding on surface phytoplankton, krill descend to deeper water and excrete carbon-rich pellets that sink, carrying carbon into the deep ocean, where it can be stored for centuries.", "why": "Traces the full causal chain—feed at the surface, descend, excrete sinking carbon-rich pellets that reach the deep ocean—which is precisely \"how\" krill store carbon."}, {"text": "In a 2022 study, oceanographer Marcus Feld estimated that Southern Ocean krill sequester about 23 million tons of carbon each year.", "why": "Gives the quantity of carbon stored, answering \"how much\" rather than explaining the process by which krill store it.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "As krill populations decline with the retreat of sea ice, the Southern Ocean is likely sequestering less carbon than it once did.", "why": "The notes report a population decline but never establish a resulting drop in total sequestration; the claim overstates the notes and describes a trend rather than the storage mechanism.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Antarctic krill are small crustaceans that feed on phytoplankton near the ocean surface.", "why": "Describes only the first step, surface feeding, and omits the descent and excretion that actually move carbon into the deep ocean.", "tag": "too-specific"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-rhs-a4", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["Urban heat islands form when city surfaces such as asphalt absorb and re-emit heat, raising local temperatures.", "In a 2020 study, urban planner Dana Reyes mapped summer temperatures across a large North American city.", "Neighborhoods with dense tree canopy were on average 4.2°C cooler than neighborhoods with little canopy.", "Lower-income neighborhoods in the city had, on average, less tree canopy than wealthier ones.", "Reyes recommended targeted tree planting in the city's low-canopy neighborhoods.", "Newly planted trees take 10 to 20 years to provide substantial shade."]}, "stem": "The student wants to explain why the cooling benefits of Reyes's recommended planting would not be immediate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "In Reyes's study, neighborhoods with dense tree canopy were on average 4.2°C cooler than neighborhoods with little canopy.", "why": "Emphasizes how large the cooling payoff is, which points away from the goal of explaining why the benefit would be delayed.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Reyes recommended planting trees in the city's low-canopy neighborhoods, many of which are lower-income.", "why": "Describes the recommendation and an equity dimension but says nothing about the timing of the benefits.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Because newly planted trees take 10 to 20 years to provide substantial shade, the cooling benefits of Reyes's plan would take years to materialize.", "why": "Cites the note that new trees \"take 10 to 20 years to provide substantial shade,\" directly explaining why the plan's cooling would be delayed."}, {"text": "Urban heat islands form when surfaces such as asphalt absorb and re-emit heat, driving up local temperatures.", "why": "Defines the underlying phenomenon as background and does not address the timing of the planting's benefits.", "tag": "too-broad"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-rhs-b1", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["Kernza is the trademarked grain of intermediate wheatgrass, a perennial crop developed by researchers at the Land Institute in Kansas.", "Unlike annual wheat, which must be replanted every year, Kernza plants produce grain for several consecutive seasons.", "Kernza's roots can grow more than ten feet deep, anchoring soil against erosion and storing carbon underground.", "The crop's per-acre grain yields are currently less than a quarter of those of annual wheat.", "Some bakeries and breweries have begun featuring Kernza in breads and beers, citing its sweet, nutty flavor."]}, "stem": "The student wants to present a tradeoff that farmers considering Kernza must weigh. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "Because Kernza plants produce grain for several consecutive seasons, farmers who grow the crop do not need to replant it every year the way they would annual wheat.", "why": "True to the second note, but it presents only a benefit of Kernza; it never weighs that benefit against a cost, so no tradeoff is presented.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Kernza, the trademarked grain of a perennial wheatgrass developed at the Land Institute in Kansas, has begun appearing in breads and beers prized for a sweet, nutty flavor.", "why": "Accurately introduces the crop and its culinary appeal, but introducing Kernza is an adjacent goal; nothing here sets competing considerations against each other for farmers to weigh.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Kernza's deep roots anchor soil against erosion and store carbon underground, but the crop currently yields less than a quarter as much grain per acre as annual wheat—an environmental benefit that farmers must weigh against smaller harvests.", "why": "Pairs the environmental benefit from the third note (deep roots that anchor soil and store carbon) with the cost from the fourth note (yields under a quarter of annual wheat's) and explicitly frames them as considerations farmers must balance—exactly the requested tradeoff."}, {"text": "Kernza's per-acre yields are currently less than a quarter of annual wheat's, making the crop unprofitable for farmers regardless of its environmental benefits.", "why": "The notes report low current yields but never state that Kernza is unprofitable 'regardless of its environmental benefits'; this overstates one side and dismisses the other instead of presenting a tradeoff.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2},
{"id": "rw-rhs-b2", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["The Federal Theatre Project (1935–1939) was a US government program created to employ theater professionals during the Great Depression.", "Among its productions were Living Newspapers: plays that dramatized current social issues using headlines, statistics, and other documentary sources.", "One-Third of a Nation, a Living Newspaper about substandard urban housing, opened in New York City in 1938.", "Its set included a four-story tenement that appeared to catch fire onstage during each performance.", "The production was seen by more than 200,000 people in New York.", "Some members of Congress argued that Living Newspapers promoted political viewpoints, and the project's funding ended in 1939."]}, "stem": "The student wants to introduce One-Third of a Nation to an audience unfamiliar with Living Newspapers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "One-Third of a Nation, which opened in New York City in 1938, was a Living Newspaper, a type of play that dramatized current social issues—in this case, substandard urban housing—using headlines, statistics, and other documentary sources.", "why": "Names the play and defines the unfamiliar term at first mention ('a type of play that dramatized current social issues...using headlines, statistics, and other documentary sources'), which is precisely what an audience unfamiliar with Living Newspapers requires."}, {"text": "One-Third of a Nation, a 1938 Living Newspaper about substandard urban housing, featured a four-story tenement that appeared to catch fire onstage during each performance.", "why": "Uses 'Living Newspaper' as though the audience already knows the term; it supplies vivid production details but never defines the genre, so it fails the unfamiliar-audience requirement of the goal.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "The Federal Theatre Project, a US government program created to employ theater professionals during the Great Depression, produced plays known as Living Newspapers.", "why": "Provides general background on the project and names the genre but never mentions One-Third of a Nation, the play the student wants to introduce.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "Funding for the Federal Theatre Project ended in 1939, after some members of Congress argued that Living Newspapers promoted political viewpoints.", "why": "Accurately reports the project's defunding, but the political controversy neither introduces One-Third of a Nation nor defines the genre for an unfamiliar audience.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 0},
{"id": "rw-rhs-b3", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["Igor Stravinsky's ballet The Rite of Spring, with choreography by Vaslav Nijinsky, premiered in Paris on May 29, 1913.", "Reviews and memoirs from the period describe audience members shouting, whistling, and scuffling during the performance.", "The premiere is frequently described as a riot in histories of modern dance.", "Some recent scholars note that no arrests were documented and that the performance continued to its end.", "These scholars suggest that memoirs written years later may have exaggerated the disturbance as the ballet's fame grew."]}, "stem": "The student wants to present the traditional account of the premiere along with a recent challenge to that account. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "The premiere of The Rite of Spring is frequently described as a riot in histories of modern dance, and reviews from the period describe audience members shouting, whistling, and scuffling.", "why": "Elaborates only the traditional riot account; the recent scholarly challenge described in the last two notes never appears, so half the goal goes unaccomplished.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Because no arrests were documented and the performance continued to its end, memoirs describing a riot at the premiere of The Rite of Spring exaggerated the disturbance.", "why": "States as settled fact what the notes hedge as something scholars 'suggest' 'may have' happened, and it replaces the traditional account with a verdict against it rather than presenting the two accounts side by side.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Premiering in Paris on May 29, 1913, The Rite of Spring paired Igor Stravinsky's score with choreography by Vaslav Nijinsky.", "why": "Accurate background about the ballet's creators and premiere date, but it says nothing about either account of the audience's behavior.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Although the premiere of The Rite of Spring is frequently described as a riot, some recent scholars suggest that memoirs written years later may have exaggerated the disturbance.", "why": "'Frequently described as a riot' delivers the traditional account, and 'some recent scholars suggest...may have exaggerated' delivers the challenge with the notes' hedging intact—both halves of the stated goal."}], "answer": 3},
{"id": "rw-rhs-b4", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["Nixtamalization, developed by Indigenous peoples of Mesoamerica thousands of years ago, involves cooking and steeping maize in an alkaline solution such as limewater.", "The process softens the kernels' hulls and makes the grain easier to grind.", "It also frees the niacin (vitamin B3) that is naturally bound in maize, allowing the human body to absorb the vitamin.", "After maize was introduced to Europe in the 1500s, it became a staple crop in some regions, but nixtamalization was not adopted along with it.", "Pellagra, a disease caused by niacin deficiency, became widespread in some European regions where diets depended heavily on maize."]}, "stem": "The student wants to explain why pellagra became widespread in some maize-dependent European regions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "Nixtamalization softens maize kernels' hulls and makes the grain easier to grind.", "why": "True to the second note, but this is the process's mechanical benefit; ease of grinding has no connection to niacin deficiency or pellagra.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Because Europeans who adopted maize as a staple did not also adopt nixtamalization—the process that frees the grain's bound niacin—their maize-heavy diets left them vulnerable to the niacin deficiency that causes pellagra.", "why": "Chains the decisive notes into a full explanation: maize became a European staple without nixtamalization (fourth note), the process that frees bound niacin (third note), so maize-heavy diets produced the niacin deficiency that causes pellagra (fifth note)."}, {"text": "Pellagra, a disease caused by niacin deficiency, became widespread in some European regions in which diets depended heavily on maize.", "why": "Restates the very phenomenon the student wants explained—where and among whom pellagra spread—without supplying any cause for it.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Thousands of years before maize reached Europe, Indigenous peoples of Mesoamerica had developed nixtamalization, cooking and steeping the grain in an alkaline solution such as limewater.", "why": "Accurate history of the technique's origin, but it never connects nixtamalization's absence in Europe to niacin or to pellagra, so it explains nothing about the disease's spread.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-rhs-c1", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["Tiktaalik roseae is an extinct animal whose fossils were discovered in the Canadian Arctic in 2004.", "It lived during the Late Devonian period, roughly 375 million years ago.", "Like fish, Tiktaalik had scales, gills, and fins.", "Unlike typical fish, it had a mobile neck, sturdy ribs, and bones resembling a wrist inside its front fins.", "These wrist-like bones could have supported the animal's weight in shallow water or on land.", "Paleontologists regard Tiktaalik as a transitional form between fish and four-limbed land vertebrates."]}, "stem": "The student wants to emphasize the traits that set Tiktaalik apart from typical fish. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "Tiktaalik, an extinct animal discovered in the Canadian Arctic in 2004, lived roughly 375 million years ago.", "why": "Accurately reports the discovery and date but names no anatomical trait, so it does nothing to distinguish Tiktaalik from typical fish.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Unlike typical fish, Tiktaalik had a mobile neck, sturdy ribs, and wrist-like bones within its front fins.", "why": "Directly names the three features the notes list as distinguishing Tiktaalik from typical fish ('Unlike typical fish, it had a mobile neck, sturdy ribs, and bones resembling a wrist'), fulfilling the goal exactly."}, {"text": "Like fish, Tiktaalik had scales, gills, and fins, features it retained from the animals it descended from.", "why": "These are the traits Tiktaalik shared with fish, the opposite of the requested set-apart features.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Because it had wrist-like bones in its fins, Tiktaalik could walk on land as easily as it swam.", "why": "Overstates the hedged note that the bones 'could have supported the animal's weight in shallow water or on land'; 'as easily as it swam' is not supported.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1},
{"id": "rw-rhs-c2", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["In 1841, American essayist Ralph Waldo Emerson published the essay \"Self-Reliance.\"", "Emerson's essay urges individuals to trust their own instincts rather than conform to social expectations.", "In 1849, American writer Henry David Thoreau published the essay \"Resistance to Civil Government.\"", "Thoreau's essay urges individuals to withhold their support from a government they consider unjust.", "Both essays are associated with the Transcendentalist movement.", "Scholars note that Emerson emphasizes personal self-trust, whereas Thoreau extends such ideas to political conduct."]}, "stem": "The student wants to emphasize a distinction between the central arguments of the two essays. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "\"Self-Reliance\" (1841) and \"Resistance to Civil Government\" (1849) are both essays associated with the Transcendentalist movement.", "why": "Stresses what the essays share rather than how their arguments differ, so it pursues the opposite of the goal.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Whereas Thoreau's \"Self-Reliance\" urges readers to trust their instincts, Emerson's \"Resistance to Civil Government\" urges them to oppose an unjust government.", "why": "Swaps the authors and titles: 'Self-Reliance' is Emerson's and 'Resistance to Civil Government' is Thoreau's, so the attribution contradicts the notes.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Emerson's \"Self-Reliance,\" published in 1841, appeared eight years before Thoreau's \"Resistance to Civil Government.\"", "why": "Presents a difference in publication dates, not a distinction between the essays' arguments, which the goal requires.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Emerson's essay urges readers to trust their own instincts, whereas Thoreau's calls on them to withhold support from a government they consider unjust.", "why": "Contrasts the two central arguments with correct attribution ('Emerson's essay urges... trust their own instincts'; 'Thoreau's essay urges... withhold their support'), accomplishing the goal."}], "answer": 3}
);
